# React architecture

## General principles

- Prefer the simplest architecture that solves the demonstrated problem.
- Follow existing project conventions unless they create a concrete maintainability problem.
- Do not introduce abstractions just to make the code look more layered or enterprise-ready.
- Every module, component, hook, and service should have a clear responsibility.
- Separate concerns when doing so makes behavior easier to understand, test, or change.

## Components

- Use function components and JSX/TSX.
- Keep UI rendering separate from complex business rules and data transformation when this improves readability or reuse.
- Extract a component when it has a distinct responsibility, meaningful reuse, or an independently understandable interface.
- Do not extract every JSX fragment into a separate component.
- Avoid components with many unrelated responsibilities or deeply nested conditional rendering.
- Keep state as close as practical to the components that use it.
- Lift state up only when multiple components genuinely need to share it.
- Avoid long chains of props through components that do not use the passed values.

## Hooks and effects

- Follow the Rules of Hooks.
- Do not use useEffect to calculate values that can be derived during rendering.
- Do not use useEffect merely to respond to a user event; handle the event in its event handler.
- Use effects for synchronization with external systems, subscriptions, timers, or imperative APIs when appropriate.
- Clean up subscriptions, timers, and other resources created by effects.
- Do not introduce useMemo or useCallback by default. Use them when there is a concrete reason.
- Extract a custom hook when it encapsulates reusable stateful behavior or substantially clarifies a component.

## Data and business logic

- Keep API access and data transformation out of presentation components when doing so clarifies responsibilities.
- Reuse existing API clients and shared data modules before introducing new ones.
- Treat @defi-dna/data as an existing project dependency: inspect its exports and intended responsibilities before duplicating its functionality.
- Keep domain-specific calculations in named, testable functions when practical.
- Do not invent a generic repository, service, or factory layer without a concrete use case.

## Routing

- Follow the existing React Router DOM v6 setup.
- Keep route definitions and page-level composition easy to discover.
- Do not change URLs, route nesting, navigation behavior, or route guards during unrelated refactoring.
- Separate route configuration from page implementation only when it improves clarity.

## File organization

- Group files by meaningful feature or domain when that fits the existing application.
- Use shared modules for genuinely shared functionality, not as a dumping ground.
- Avoid circular dependencies.
- Do not move many files at once without a migration plan and verified import updates.
